      "voice_kokoro": "am_onyx",
      "narration_text": "The multiplication is what got me — six functions at ninety percent each compound to a fifty-three-percent batch yield, not ninety. I want to feel that capability-versus-manufacturability trade-off. Can you (1) derive the yield curve as the number of functions and the per-function quality vary; (2) show how single-function designs like Doxil and radioligands sidestep the compounding; and (3) work out what per-function quality a six-function particle needs to reach a ninety-percent batch pass rate?",
